      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">The <strong>historic neighborhoods around the Athens courthouse square and Athens State University</strong> contain some of the most architecturally interesting homes in Limestone County — 1920s–1950s construction with original hardwood floors, crawlspace foundations, and the moisture history that comes with seven decades of North Alabama humidity. We've cleaned hardwood floors in Athens that predate World War II, inspected crawlspaces with mold that has been growing for years without the homeowner's knowledge, and restored historic homes after plumbing failures that would have caused catastrophic damage without rapid response. This is work that requires experience — not a franchise crew on its first visit to the neighborhood.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;"><strong>Forrest Hills, Peppertree, Ashley Park, and the established subdivisions</strong> north and west of downtown Athens are the core of BamaClean's Athens residential business — family homes from the 1980s and 1990s with wall-to-wall carpet in bedrooms and living areas, tile in kitchens and bathrooms that has accumulated years of grout discoloration, and the kind of accumulated allergen load in carpet that builds when a family with pets lives in a home for a decade without professional cleaning. These are our most common Athens jobs, and we know these neighborhoods well.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">The <strong>newer subdivisions south of I-65 and along US-72</strong> — Stone Bridge, Limestone Springs, and the developments that have grown rapidly with the MTMUS workforce — present a different profile. Newer construction, slab foundations in many cases, and the rapid buildout pace that doesn't always allow adequate moisture management. We're seeing mold in Athens homes built in 2018–2022 that results from construction moisture that wasn't properly dried before finishes were applied. If your new Athens home has a musty smell, it's worth investigating.</p>
      <p>The <strong>Elk River and rural Limestone County communities</strong> south and west of Athens — Tanner, Elkmont, and the agricultural areas of the county — have homes with the highest radon risk in our entire service area. Limestone bedrock is close to the surface in these areas, and radon levels in homes on slab foundations with limited sub-slab barriers can be substantially above the EPA action level. We test and document these levels for homeowners who want to understand their exposure.</p>
    `,
